Foresight: what it changed in 2025
One page for finance. Every range is a 95% interval unless
it says otherwise.

SIMULATED. No retention calls are on record. The retention
lines come from a simulation in which a call keeps 25% of
would-be leavers (the brief's assumption). They show what the
test will report, not what it has found.

Retention calls: 12 cohorts; of each top 40, 20 called,
20 held out at random
  Left, held out / called  31.2% / 19.2%
  Calls cut leaving by     12.1 points (4.2 to 19.6)
  Save rate                39% of would-be leavers (13% to 63%)
  Net per call             $255 ($29 to $469)
  A year of 480 calls      $122,000 ($14,000 to $225,000)
  Caution: 12 of the 35 cohorts planned. At a 25% save rate
  a year this size finds the effect 38% of the time, and
  overstates it when it does.

Stock: ordering to the forecast, 190 product x region series
  Point forecast against last year's month   $409,000 a year
  Range forecast against a padded last year  $384,000 a year
  As the cost assumptions move: $227,000 to $772,000

Urgent tickets: 407 in 2025, hours to first read
                          median  90% within
  arrival order              2.0        19.4
  urgent words first         0.6        16.5
  v0.8's ranking             0.3         0.6
  Assumed desk: 7:00 to 19:00, a ticket every 40 minutes

Not claimed here
  Accounts kept by the forecast or by triage; the retention
  effect on accounts outside the top 40; any effect of calls
  on accounts that were staying anyway.

Asked of finance
  Keep 20 of each list uncalled until cohort 35 (September
  2027), read the test once a year, and judge the calls on the
  final reading. Until then, cost the calls at the brief's 25%.
